---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../../content';
const site = siteFromHost();
const path = "/compare/postscript-vs-activecampaign";
const title = "Postscript vs ActiveCampaign for Shopify: channel and CRM ownership";
const desc = "Compare Shopify messaging and CRM responsibilities, current SMS scope and Postscript Email eligibility before changing your stack.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Can Postscript replace ActiveCampaign?",
    "answer": "Only after mapping the jobs you actually use. Replacing Shopify messages does not establish a replacement for sales pipelines, lead nurture or existing integrations. Postscript now advertises Email, so its old SMS-only description is also incomplete."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does ActiveCampaign support two-way SMS?",
    "answer": "Yes. Its SMS Inbox documentation describes replies to automated messages and broadcasts, with dedicated registered numbers in supported countries and inbox permissions. Check account and country eligibility rather than assuming every account can use it."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should both platforms send cart messages?",
    "answer": "Assign one owner per channel and journey. If both platforms remain, demonstrate purchase exits, opt-outs and audience exclusions before enabling overlapping automations."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a></nav>
<header class="max-w-4xl"><p class="editorial-kicker">Shopify decision guide</p><h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1><p class="mt-7 text-xl">{desc}</p></header>
<article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
<p><em>Scope checked November 25, 2026. Supplier documentation supports product scope, not comparative performance. Exercises below are proposed and unperformed.</em></p>
<h2>Start with the work ActiveCampaign already owns</h2>
<p>A Shopify merchant with wholesale inquiries may use ActiveCampaign for both retail lifecycle messages and sales follow-up. Export an inventory of active automations, entry conditions, deal dependencies and downstream integrations. Mark which are needed for consumer retention and which belong to a sales process. Removing a messaging vendor is a different project from rebuilding a pipeline; a contact export cannot preserve the latter.</p>
<h2>Correct the old channel assumptions</h2>
<p><a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/launch">Postscript’s launch page</a> announces Email alongside its SMS offering, with a waitlist link. Treat Email eligibility and the supported migration path as account-specific questions before promising consolidation. Its advertised scope does not prove that your templates, automations or CRM work transfer. Do not base a replacement decision on the obsolete claim that Postscript has no email product.</p>
<h2>ActiveCampaign is not limited to one-way reminder texts</h2>
<p>The <a class="link" href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/21563461536284-Use-the-SMS-Inbox-for-two-way-text-messaging">ActiveCampaign SMS Inbox documentation</a> covers replies to automated and broadcast messages, subject to number, country and permission requirements. This removes an unsupported reason to declare Postscript the winner. Evaluate who receives replies, how a support handoff works and whether the store’s sending region is supported. Feature availability is not comparative deliverability or conversion evidence.</p>
<h2>Map Shopify events before splitting the stack</h2>
<p>The <a class="link" href="https://help.activecampaign.com/hc/en-us/articles/115000202150-Connect-your-Shopify-store-to-ActiveCampaign">Shopify integration guide</a> describes purchase data, segmentation and automation, with plan-dependent historical sync. Make a field map for order, checkout and marketing permission data used by each live journey. A customer existing in both tools does not establish equivalent event timing or consent. If Postscript owns SMS while ActiveCampaign keeps email, identify the actual supported connection and an owner for resolving mismatched states.</p>
<h2>Proposed exercise: wholesale buyer becomes a retail shopper</h2>
<p>This exercise has not been performed. Use a test profile representing a wholesale lead who later places a Shopify consumer order. Inspect whether a retail welcome offer is appropriate, whether the wholesale follow-up remains assigned, and which purchase exits apply. Repeat with a phone number but no SMS permission. The useful result is a written entry rule for each audience, not a universal requirement to exclude every wholesale buyer from retail marketing.</p>
<h2>Rehearse a cart reply and completed purchase</h2>
<p>In an unperformed rehearsal, abandon a test cart, reply with a product question, then complete the order before the next scheduled touch. Observe inbox assignment, order ingestion and cancellation of queued recovery messages in each intended sender. Also check a signup welcome, a post-purchase education message and a winback branch based on purchase history. Document observed delays and resolve unsafe overlap before enabling the journey; no fixed delay or arbitrary trial duration guarantees safety.</p>
<h2>Compare the cost of the architecture you intend to keep</h2>
<p>Obtain current quotes for the same retained contacts, sending countries, email volume, SMS segments, support and required add-ons. Keep continuing CRM costs separate from messaging replacement costs. Include integration maintenance and rebuilding any sales automation that would otherwise disappear. Neither an old starting price nor a claimed share of SMS revenue determines whether two vendors are justified. There is no supported savings or recovery-uplift estimate on this page.</p>
<h2>Choose an ownership plan before a vendor winner</h2>
<p>Keep ActiveCampaign where documented sales or nurture dependencies make it useful. Evaluate Postscript for the Shopify messaging work you can demonstrate in an eligible account. Consolidate only when the replacement covers those requirements and the team can operate it. Before cutover, record sender authentication, audience permission, opt-out propagation, queued-message handling and a rollback owner. Preserve the current working sender until the replacement’s entry and exit behavior has been checked without duplicate production sends.</p>
</article>
      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-[#24170f]">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/drip-vs-activecampaign">Drip vs ActiveCampaign</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/activecampaign-vs-klaviyo">ActiveCampaign vs Klaviyo</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/postscript-vs-mailchimp">Postscript vs Mailchimp</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/supplements">Email apps for supplement stores</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Postscript vs ActiveCampaign FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
